    <h2 style="margin-top:0">Where WordPress is the right call</h2>
    <p>WordPress is strong for publishers: news sites, magazines, multi-author blogs, and content memberships. Its editor and user roles are what it was built for. If your site exists mainly to publish writing, use WordPress, ideally with a managed host and a maintainer you trust.</p>

    <h2>Where WordPress costs you leads</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Slower pages.</strong> Page builders such as Elementor and Divi load hundreds of kilobytes of unused code. Typical service-business WordPress sites score 30&ndash;65 on Google's speed test for phones, and reaching 90 takes heavy caching and developer time.</li>
      <li><strong>Security that costs leads.</strong> WordPress is by far the most-targeted platform on the web. If your site is defaced, redirected, or dropped from Google, your phone stops ringing, and a maintenance retainer lowers that risk without removing it.</li>
      <li><strong>Plugin overload.</strong> A typical service-business site runs 15 to 30 plugins, each with its own updates, conflicts, and security holes. Any update can break the site while a customer is filling out your form.</li>
      <li><strong>Generic local search detail.</strong> Yoast, Rank Math, and similar plugins produce the same business details for Google (schema) on every WordPress site in your trade. Google's map results (the "local pack") increasingly reward unique pages.</li>
      <li><strong>A chain of vendors.</strong> Hosting, theme, page builder, backups, security, SEO, and forms each bring a subscription and updates, and each can break on its own. With me, one person is accountable for all of it.</li>
    </ul>

